<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samanid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samanids, Samanid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samanid Empire was a Persian dynasty that ruled Khurasan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> in the ninth and tenth centuries, initially as governors under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. Its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> became a centre of the revival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-language">Persian language</a> and literature. Accounts of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> list the Samanids among the autonomous successor states, while accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> note their role on the frontier where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> entered the Islamic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samanid-empire.jpg" alt="Samanid Empire" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original file by Ro4444, edited by me · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Samanid_amirate_at_the_death_of_Nasr_II,_943.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
